Follow a jump of the echo's delay that nothing marked at once, once the estimate is settled

When the delay between what is played and what comes back in the microphone jumps with
nothing marked (no change of devices, no restart or move of a stream: a device's glitch,
the sound server's own buffering; tools/call-lab/device-echo.sh jump:<ms>), AEC3's delay
estimator kept the old delay until estimates at the new one outnumbered it in its
histogram of the last 250 (MatchedFilterLagAggregator: 126 estimates at the new delay,
half a second at the least and longer with speech, which gives none in its pauses).
Meanwhile its filters stayed aligned to the old delay and the other side's voice came
back to them.

Once the estimate is settled (a refined delay, and 5 s since the estimator was last reset
hard), 32 lag estimates in a row more than two blocks (8 ms) from the current delay and
within a block of each other, with none near the current delay between them, now move
it there at once: the aggregator's histograms start over from those estimates, at the
lag most of them gave, which is a refined estimate, and the render delay buffer aligns
to it as to any new delay (EchoPathVariability kNewDetectedDelay, as before).

Around a marked change of devices nothing changes. The realign of patches 0010, 0014 and
0016 (and a call's start, and a render buffer overrun) resets the estimator hard, which
starts the 5 s hold-off: the delays a new device settles through are followed exactly as
before. An echo still seen at the old delay among the new estimates (an echo coming both
ways) keeps the old delay, and a drift of a lag or two (clock drift) never reaches the
two blocks.

Tested in matched_filter_lag_aggregator_unittest.cc: a settled estimate follows a jump
after the 32nd estimate at the new delay; within the hold-off after a hard reset it
follows only after the 126 as before; estimates at the old delay among the new keep it.
Not measured in a call. To measure: device-echo-cases.sh jump (60 to 180 ms and back) for
the time the other side comes back after each jump and its level, and last night's echo
and headset series with output changes (marked) for no more echo than 8127.4 there;
interleaved with the library without it. The log says "echo delay jumped ... estimate
moved at once" at each jump followed this way.

